<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:id="@+id/widget_root"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:padding="16dp"
    android:background="@drawable/widget_background"
    android:gravity="center">

    <!-- ADDED 10 Oct 2026 (t091) - the static title every other widget already
         carries. This layout was the only one of the ten whose FIRST line was a
         value (the medication name) rather than a title, which is why it read
         unlike its nine siblings: Refills/Last Test/Appointments/DoxyPEP all
         open with a 16sp bold label of what the widget IS, then the value.

         The title is static and carries no user data, so it stays visible at
         every privacy tier - the same reason the other nine titles are static
         literals rather than provider-set text. The medication name below it
         is the identifying part, and the provider already replaces that with
         the redacted line and hides widget_next_dose at the Redacted tier
         (NextDoseWidgetProvider.java). Nothing about that path changes: this
         view is never hidden, so widgetRedactedRender.test.js's "every id it
         hides exists in its layout" assertion is untouched.

         Label text matches the picker label and the receiver_label_next_dose
         string ("Next Dose"), so the tile says the same thing in the picker,
         on the home screen and in the widget's own settings entry. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/widget_title"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:text="Next Dose"
        android:textSize="16sp"
        android:textColor="@color/widget_text_primary"
        android:textStyle="bold" />

    <!-- The value slot: which medication the time below belongs to. Promoted to
         18sp so the hierarchy matches the other nine widgets, where the second
         line is the headline and the first is only the label. It was 16sp,
         identical to the title, so the two lines competed instead of reading
         as label-then-value. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/widget_med_name"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="8dp"
        android:text="Medication"
        android:textSize="18sp"
        android:textColor="@color/widget_text_primary"
        android:textStyle="bold"
        android:maxLines="2"
        android:ellipsize="end" />

    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/widget_next_dose"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="4dp"
        android:text="Next dose: --:--"
        android:textSize="14sp"
        android:textColor="@color/widget_text_muted" />

    <!-- CHANGED 2 Oct 2026 - a real Chronometer rather than a JS interval.
         The widget has updatePeriodMillis="0" because a medicine schedule is
         not a minute-resolution thing, so without this the countdown would tick
         once and then lie for the next hour. Chronometer is updated by the
         system UI, so it needs no alarm, no wake lock and no app process - the
         whole reason not to drive this from a setInterval on the JS side.

         setChronometerCountDown (rather than counting up) is what makes it read
         "in 4h 12m" towards the deadline instead of counting away from it.

         CHANGED 4 Oct 2026 - clickable/focusable stated explicitly. The root
         LinearLayout carries the click PendingIntent, and a Chronometer extends
         TextView; a TextView only consumes touches when something makes it
         clickable, so these are false by default. Stated because the failure is
         invisible if a later edit adds android:clickable="true": the countdown
         would become the one region of the widget that does nothing on tap.

         NOTE this comment sits ABOVE the tag, not inside it. An XML comment is
         not legal inside a tag's attribute list, and putting one there fails
         :app:parseDebugLocalResources with "Element type must be followed by
         either attribute specifications, '>' or '/>'". Same shape as the JSX
         `//`-in-children-position bug this repo has already recorded. -->
    <Chronometer
        android:id="@+id/widget_countdown"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="4dp"
        android:visibility="gone"
        android:textSize="14sp"
        android:textColor="@color/widget_text_primary"
        android:clickable="false"
        android:focusable="false" />
</LinearLayout>